[inference]
contract:
  input:
    ticker: string
    industry: string
    company_board: object
    source_evidence: array
    search_log: array
  output:
    industry_drivers: array
    self_regulatory_bodies: array
    company_fit: array
    frame_conflicts: array
    implication_for_falstaffian: string
    implication_for_gorilla: string
    implication_for_thesis: string
    claim_sources: array
    excluded_sources: array
    data_gaps: array
visibility: Public
---
{# goal: Build a narrative outside view of what drives profitability and growth in the subject's industry from scholarly, case-study, book and self-regulatory sources, then read the company's self-view against it. Advisory: never a gate or a score. #}
[inference]
temperature = 0.2
work_effort = "medium"
verbosity = "standard"

You are building the outside view on an industry's business drivers: the frameworks researchers, business-school cases, serious books and the industry's own self-regulatory organizations use to explain how firms in this industry turn capital into profit and growth. This is the analyst reading around the company to see how everything moves around it (MAIA Guidebook, Idea Generation: maintenance research). It is advisory: it never blocks the pipeline and never produces a score. The company's own filings remain the anchor for what the company is; this view tests that self-view against how the industry is understood by people who study it.

## Company: {{ ticker }}
## Industry under study: {{ industry }}

## Company self-view (the exact accepted COMPANY `company_board` object, not a paraphrased replacement)
{{ company_board | tojson }}

If this is a shortened reconstruction rather than the predecessor's actual complete object, do not treat the handoff as valid: report the missing board identity and request the original object before synthesizing company fit.

## Short extracts from outside-view sources
{{ source_evidence | tojson }}

## Outside-view search log (queries, providers, counts, failures)
{{ search_log | tojson }}

## Admissible sources (the only ones that may support a driver)

1. Scholarly publications with a resolvable identity (DOI, arXiv ID or OpenAlex ID), found through OpenAlex, arXiv, Semantic Scholar or Google Scholar.
2. Business-school case studies and faculty research from the case publishers' own sites, cited by publisher and case number or URL.
3. Books from academic or established trade publishers, found through Google Books, cited by title, author, publisher and year (ISBN when shown). Quote a book only from retrieved text; otherwise cite it without quoting.
4. Industry self-regulatory organizations and standard-setting bodies for this industry, from their own published standards, rules, statistics or reports.
5. Substack publications whose author is identified and whose publication shows more than 1,000 subscribers on the retrieved page.

Never admissible, and never searched: forum posts, Reddit, general web search results, search-engine answer boxes, and consultant or industry-association reports that are not themselves in tier 1–4 or cited by a tier-1–3 source. A source that bears on nothing about this industry's economics is not evidence: drop it without listing, classifying or counting it.

In one bounded pass:
1. Name only drivers whose mechanism is supported by an admissible source for the specified industry: cost structure, capital intensity, pricing power, cyclicality, entry barriers, customer concentration, regulation and standards, technology substitution, working-capital norms. A study from an adjacent category may supply a **bounded analogy to test**, not a measured driver of this industry or this company; label its source population and limitation in `industry_drivers`. Give each driver or analogy its mechanism and real source keys. A single applicable source is `single_source`; two studies of different populations or mechanisms do not automatically make `corroborated`; source disagreement is `contested`.
2. List the self-regulatory organizations and standard-setting bodies you found for this industry, with what each governs and why it bears on the industry's economics (licensing, capacity rules, product standards, published industry statistics). Say `none found` when none was found; that is a gap, not a finding about the industry.
3. For each driver, state whether period- and definition-matched company evidence shows the **mechanism itself**, citing the original source keys carried by the COMPANY board: `shows`, `contradicts`, or `no evidence yet`. Product presence, a premium label, 40% wholesale exposure or an overall gross margin does not by itself show brand preference, retailer price pass-through or manufacturer pricing power. If the outside study concerns another category and no corresponding company observation is held, use `no evidence yet` even when the company participates in that channel. Financial Signposts may challenge or support a matched mechanism, but DSO/DPO and a retailer's inventory description are not wholesale price observations.
4. Record `frame_conflicts` only where a cited, applicable outside source and a cited company claim make incompatible assertions on the same mechanism and scope. Missing mechanism evidence is a question, not a discovered conflict. These conflicts feed the Falstaffian rotation.
5. State what this outside view changes for the Falstaffian rotation, GORILLA's Obvious Problem and Choke Point, and the thesis's Business Franchise pillar and Risks, or "no supported change".

Cite `source_evidence` by its citation key for every driver, body and conflict. An extract is a lead until checked against the source it was extracted from. Record in `excluded_sources` only admissible-looking candidates you rejected (for example an unresolvable DOI or a Substack below the subscriber floor) with the reason; do not list boilerplate or inadmissible source types. Record provider failures, empty tiers and unresolved identities in `data_gaps`. Never invent a driver, a citation or a subscriber count.

Return JSON only with exactly this shape: `{"industry_drivers":[], "self_regulatory_bodies":[], "company_fit":[], "frame_conflicts":[], "implication_for_falstaffian":"...", "implication_for_gorilla":"...", "implication_for_thesis":"...", "claim_sources":[], "excluded_sources":[], "data_gaps":[]}`. Populate `claim_sources` with real citation keys used by each claim, not new citations; empty arrays and `no supported change` are allowed when sources do not support a judgment.
